# 0006. Candidate profiles: immutable, schema-validated JSON revisions

- Status: Proposed (not approved)
- Date: 2026-11-13

A profile is a named container. Its content is a sequence of immutable revisions, each one
JSON document validated against a versioned JSON Schema. Match results point to the exact
revision they used.

## Technical details

**Problem.**
- SPEC §5 asks for a structured profile derived from your CV.
- v1 can use a static, hand-curated profile, but the design must allow several profiles
  and revisions later.
- Nothing candidate-specific may be hardcoded in the matcher.
- Reproducible matching (SPEC §6.2) needs the profile used for a result to never change
  afterwards.

**Tables** (migration `0003_profiles.sql`):
```sql
candidate_profiles (id bigserial PK, name text NOT NULL UNIQUE, created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now())
profile_revisions  (id bigserial PK, profile_id → candidate_profiles, revision int NOT NULL,
                    schema_version int NOT NULL, data jsonb NOT NULL, created_at,
                    UNIQUE (profile_id, revision))
```
- The current revision is the one with the highest `revision`. Nothing points to it, so
  nothing can go stale.
- Revisions are never updated or deleted by the application. The store package has no
  UPDATE query for this table.

**Document** (`schema_version` 1, the schema in `docs/api/profile.schema.json`):
- `experience[]`: `{title, company, start, end?, responsibilities[], achievements[],
  skills[]}`
- `seniority`, `years_of_experience`
- `skills[]`: `{name, level?, years?}`. Names are free text, and the matcher maps them to
  its taxonomy.
- `preferences`:
  - `employment_types[]`
  - `remote_only`
  - `locations[]` and `timezones[]` (an allowed UTC offset range)
  - `salary {min, currency, period}`
  - `hard_constraints[]`: which preferences are gates and which are soft.
- `extra`: a free-form object for preferences the schema doesn't know yet.

The schema is checked by the backend on `POST /profiles/{id}/revisions` and by the matcher
on every request. A change to the schema bumps `schema_version`. Older revisions keep
theirs, and the matcher supports every version it has ever accepted.

**Loading your profile.**
- You write `profiles/<name>.yaml` from your CV. It is git-ignored, and
  `profiles/example.yaml` shows the shape.
- `jobradar profile import profiles/<name>.yaml` (or the API) creates a new revision.
  When the content equals the latest revision, it is a no-op, so the import is idempotent.

**Privacy.**
- Profile data is never logged. Log lines carry the profile id and revision only.
- It stays in the local Postgres and the git-ignored file (SPEC §10).

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: Postgres jsonb, JSON Schema 2020-12 (`santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema` in Go,
  `jsonschema` in Python tests).
- Alternatives:
  - Fully relational profile (experience, skills and preference tables): enforced
    structure, but a dozen tables are always read whole, and comparing revisions becomes
    a multi-table diff.
  - A mutable single profile row: breaks reproducibility.
